# hy4-flash-bf16-f32.recipe -- hy4-flash-bf16.recipe with each Engram row's E4M3 codes taken exactly as
# the transformers port takes them in f32: scale = amax(row) / 448 kept in f32, codes = e4m3(x / scale)
# (a division, libquant's `divide=1`, not a multiply by the reciprocal), read by embed_lookup_q as
# decode(code) * scale in f32. Against the port's f32 forward (`_fp8_round_trip`, the parity
# harness's reference) every code and every scale is the port's: checked on the minis' tables (mini
# 2112 codes, gpu-mini 441792), where the bf16 table's runtime round trip (x times 1/scale) differs
# in 4.3e-4 of the codes and hy4-flash-bf16.recipe's bf16 scale moves more. The port's bf16 forward
# divides in bf16 (hy4-flash-bf16.recipe's header) and matches neither exactly.
#
#   rad-convert <Hy4-flash checkpoint> --add engram-tables.safetensors \
#       --recipe data/recipes/hy4-flash-bf16-f32.recipe -o hy4-flash-bf16-f32.rad
#
# 660 million rows at 96 codes and a 4-byte scale: 61.5 GiB of tables, 1.3 GiB above the bf16
# scale's. Any other recipe's Engram line may be swapped for this one.
#
# PATTERN                     QUANTISER  OPTIONS
blk.*.engram_table.weight     rtn        codes=fp8_e4m3 block=1x* scale=f32 rule=absmax divide=1
